          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.451,
            0.068,
            0.817,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "I’m Up and Down, and Round and Round"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.844,
            0.068,
            0.883,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": "101"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.108,
            0.889,
            0.15
          ],
          "content": "**Theorem 4:** The line joining the centre of a circle and the midpoint of a chord of the circle is perpendicular to the chord."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.222,
            0.427,
            0.435
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.246,
                0.441,
                0.325,
                0.46
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 5.12"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.454,
            0.169,
            0.878,
            0.267
          ],
          "content": "Let us see why this is true. Draw a circle with centre C (Fig. 5.12). Draw any chord AB. Let M be the midpoint of AB. We must show that CM is perpendicular to AB."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.455,
            0.27,
            0.877,
            0.329
          ],
          "content": "**Explanation:** CAB is an isosceles triangle. AB is the base and $CA = CB$ $\\mathrm{S}0,\\angle \\mathrm{A} = \\angle \\mathrm{B}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.455,
            0.334,
            0.877,
            0.373
          ],
          "content": "M is the midpoint of AB. So, $\\mathrm{AM} = \\mathrm{BM}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.455,
            0.379,
            0.877,
            0.462
          ],
          "content": "By the SAS congruence, ∆ CMA is congruent to $\\Delta  CMB.    SO , \\angle  CMB = \\angle  CMA.$ But $\\angle CMB \\pm \\angle CMA = 180^{\\circ}$ (angles on a line)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.455,
            0.467,
            0.877,
            0.508
          ],
          "content": "So, both angles are 90°. That is, CM is perpendicular to AB."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.374,
            0.567,
            0.622,
            0.595
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Exercise Set 5.3"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.616,
            0.885,
            0.678
          ],
          "content": "1. Can you explain why the converse to Theorem 4 is true, i.e., why does the perpendicular from the centre of a circle to a chord of the circle bisect the chord?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.681,
            0.884,
            0.723
          ],
          "content": "(**Hint:** Use Fig. 5.12. You are told that $\\angle CMA = \\angle CMB = 90^{\\circ}$ . You need to show that AM = BM.)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.727,
            0.886,
            0.788
          ],
          "content": "2. An isosceles triangle ABC is inscribed in a circle, with $\\mathrm{AB} = \\mathrm{AC}$ Show that the altitude from A to BC passes through the centre of the circle."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.131,
            0.794,
            0.886,
            0.859
          ],
          "content": "3. Two parallel chords of lengths 6 cm and 8 cm are on opposite sides of the centre of a circle. If the radius of the circle is 5 cm, find the distance between the midpoints of the chords."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
